<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Meeting My Friends – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Identify characters in a picture book. Phonemic Awareness: Identify environmental sounds and speech sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize and name main characters in a story about meeting friends and being polite when prompted by the teacher (e.g., when the teacher asks, "Who is this?" the student says the character's name or points while attempting to say the name). Can select the correct picture of a character who says "please" or "thank you" in a familiar story when hearing the character's name (e.g., when the teacher says "Show me Lizzie," the student picks the correct picture from multiple options). Can identify the source of an environmental or speech sound related to classroom routines or stories when supported with pictures (e.g., pointing to a picture of a friend saying "hello" or a classroom bell after hearing the sound).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage &amp; Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities &amp; Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Hello, Friends!" song with Total Physical Response (TPR). Students wave their hands and say "Hello!" to classmates. The teacher models waving and saying "Hello, friends!" while pointing to different children. This activates prior knowledge of greetings and the word "friend." (Pronunciation focus: Syllable stress in "friends," initial /f/ in "friend," and environmental sound of voices saying "hello").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher uses a friendly puppet, "Patches the Panda." The teacher says, "Hello, Patches!" and waves. Then, the teacher says, "I am your teacher. What is your name?" (pointing to self and then to the puppet, answering for the puppet: "I am Patches."). The teacher then models approaching a student, waving, and asking, "Hello! What is your name?" The teacher guides the student to respond with "I am [student's name]" or just their name. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of someone waving) "Do we say 'hello'?" (Students nod or say "yes").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to self) "Am I your teacher?" (Students nod or say "yes").</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards of characters (e.g., Lizzie, Max) from a familiar picture book about meeting friends (introduced in Lesson 1 and 2), and flashcards depicting actions like waving, playing, and saying "please" or "thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to initiate simple greetings and respond to basic personal questions about names. They will practice using polite expressions like "hello," "please," and "thank you" in interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities &amp; Teacher Actions:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays flashcards of the characters from the story (Lizzie, Max). "Look! Who is this?" (Teacher points to Lizzie). "This is Lizzie! Say 'Hello, Lizzie!'" (Choral repetition). "Is Lizzie happy?" (Students point to happy face).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of a friend asking for a toy using a speech bubble with "Please." "Lizzie wants to play! She says 'Please!'" (Teacher models "please" with an open hand gesture). "What does Lizzie say?" (Students repeat "Please!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of a friend receiving a toy with a speech bubble with "Thank you." "Max gives Lizzie the toy. Lizzie says 'Thank you!'" (Teacher models "thank you" with a nod). "What does Lizzie say now?" (Students repeat "Thank you!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays sounds of a child saying "hello" and a dog barking. "Listen! What sound is it?" (Teacher uses picture cues for "hello" and "dog"). "Is it a friend saying 'hello' or a dog?" (Students point to the correct picture and attempt to say the word). This addresses phonemic awareness of speech sounds vs. environmental sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill &amp; Content:</b> Students will practice saying "Hello, [name]!", "I am [name].", "What is your name?", "Please," and "Thank you." They will focus on clear pronunciation, especially initial /f/ in "friend," /g/ in "goodbye," and syllable stress in "friends" and "goodbye."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Echo Practice (Controlled):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of "friend," "teacher," "name," and character pictures (Lizzie, Max). The teacher says each word or phrase, and students echo chorally: "Hello!" "Friend!" "My name is..." "Please!" "Thank you!" The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /f/ in "friend" and the syllable stress in "friends."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Meet and Say" Activity (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher passes a soft toy or puppet (e.g., Patches the Panda) to a student. The teacher models: "Hello, Patches! What is your name?" The student then holds the toy and, with teacher support, says "I am [their name]." The student then passes the toy to another student, saying "Hello, [friend's name]!" (or "Hello, [student's actual name]!"). The teacher prompts "What does [student's name] say?" to elicit "I am [name]." The teacher also incorporates "please" (e.g., "Please pass the toy") and "thank you" (e.g., "Thank you, [student's name]"). (Pronunciation focus: Initial /g/ in "goodbye" will be reinforced when transitioning activities).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Polite Request &amp; Response (Pair Interaction - Guided):</b> Students are grouped into pairs (or work with a single 'buddy' plush toy). The teacher distributes small classroom items (e.g., blocks, crayons). The teacher models: "Give me the block, please." (Teacher holds out hand). "Thank you!" The teacher then prompts students to take turns practicing: Student A asks, "Block, please?" (or "Crayon, please?"). Student B gives the item and says nothing, then Student A says "Thank you!" The teacher circulates, provides cues, and encourages both parts of the interaction.</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Friendly Circle Greetings" – a simple role-play activity where students practice meeting and greeting each other, using polite expressions with minimal teacher intervention, focusing on fluency and communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities &amp; Teacher Actions:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stand in a large circle. The teacher explains the game: "We will meet our friends and say hello!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts by turning to the person next to them and modeling: "Hello, [friend's name]! I am [teacher's name]. What is your name?" The teacher guides the student to respond "I am [student's name]."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student then turns to the next person in the circle and attempts the same greeting. The teacher provides silent cues (e.g., pointing to mouth for "hello," pointing to self for "I am") and gentle reminders as needed.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then introduces the "Please and Thank You Pass." The teacher holds a colorful ball or soft toy. "Please, give me the ball." The teacher models giving it to a student and saying, "Thank you, [student's name]!" The student then passes it to another, saying "Please, [friend's name]!" and the receiver says "Thank you!" This encourages use of polite expressions in a natural, repetitive context. (Reinforce "goodbye" and its syllable stress when the activity concludes).</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Friendly Circle Greetings" and "Please and Thank You Pass" activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will participate in a simple interactive game where they greet classmates/the teacher and use polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can say "Hello" clearly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond "I am [name]" (or their name).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to ask "What is your name?" (with support).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "Please" when asking for an item.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "Thank you" when receiving an item.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces initial /f/ in "friend" intelligibly.</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of two friends greeting) "Did you say 'hello' to a friend today?" (Students give a thumbs up or down, or point to "yes" / "no" pictures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you say 'thank you' today?" (Students give a thumbs up or down).</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you say today? (e.g., "hello," "please," "thank you").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who did you say "hello" to?</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    </p><ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students speak and interact using greetings and polite phrases? The "Friendly Circle Greetings" activity seemed to engage most students.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? I need to prepare visual aids that represent these phrases for writing. Perhaps a chant with specific gestures to reinforce the connection between spoken word and action, preparing for matching words to pictures. I will also ensure specific environmental sounds related to the theme are easily distinguishable for phonemic awareness.</li>
                    </ul>
                <p></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on speaking greetings and polite expressions. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build upon this by having students recognize and trace the written forms of "hello," "please," and "thank you," and potentially match character names to pictures, moving from oral production to early literacy skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr></tbody></table>